<com:TContent ID="body" >

<h1>TReCaptcha2</h1>
<com:SinceVersion Version="3.3.1" />
<com:DocLink ClassPath="Prado.Web.UI.WebControls.TReCaptcha2" />

<p class="block-content">
<tt>TReCaptcha2</tt> displays a <a href="https://www.google.com/recaptcha">reCAPTCHA v2</a> that can be used to determine if the input is entered by a real user instead of some program. It can also prevent multiple submits of the same form either by accident, or on purpose (ie. spamming).
</p>

<p class="block-content">
The reCAPTCHA to solve (a string consisting of two separate words) displayed is automatically generated by the reCAPTCHA system at recaptcha.net. However, in order to use the services of the site you will need to register and get a public and a private API key pair, and  supply those to the reCAPTCHA control through setting the <tt>SecretKey</tt> and <tt>SiteKey</tt> properties.
</p>

<p class="block-content">
Version 2 renders each control as its own widget, and each one posts under its own field
name, so a page may carry more than one. That is a change from
<a href="?page=Controls.ReCaptcha">TReCaptcha</a>, which allows only one.
</p>

<p class="block-content">
Upon postback, user input can be validated by calling <tt>validate()</tt>. The <tt>TReCaptcha2Validator</tt> control can also be used to do validation, which provides server-side validation. Calling <tt>validate()</tt> will invalidate the token supplied, so all consecutive calls to the method - without solving a new captcha - will return false. Therefore if implementing a multi-stage input process, you must make sure that you call <tt>validate()</tt> only once, either at the end of the input process, or you store the result till the end of the processing.
</p>

<p class="block-content">
The following template shows a typical use of the <tt>TReCaptcha2</tt> control:
</p>

<com:TTextHighlighter Language="prado" CssClass="source block-content">
&lt;com:TReCaptcha2 ID="Captcha"
   SiteKey="..."
   SecretKey="..."
/>
&lt;com:TReCaptcha2Validator ControlToValidate="Captcha"
    ErrorMessage="You are challenged!" />
</com:TTextHighlighter>

<h2>Keys</h2>

<p class="block-content">
The service issues a pair of keys for a site. <tt>SiteKey</tt> is public and reaches the
browser. <tt>SecretKey</tt> stays on the server and is used to verify the answer. The
control checks both during pre-render, and a missing one raises a
<tt>TConfigurationException</tt> rather than rendering a widget that cannot work.
</p>

<h2>Appearance</h2>

<table class="parameters">
<tr><th>Property</th><th>Values</th><th>Default</th></tr>
<tr>
	<td><tt>Theme</tt></td>
	<td>The color theme of the widget, <tt>light</tt> or <tt>dark</tt>.</td>
	<td><tt>light</tt></td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td><tt>Type</tt></td>
	<td>The challenge to serve, <tt>image</tt> or <tt>audio</tt>.</td>
	<td><tt>image</tt></td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td><tt>Size</tt></td>
	<td>The widget size, <tt>normal</tt> or <tt>compact</tt>.</td>
	<td><tt>normal</tt></td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td><tt>Language</tt></td>
	<td>The language code the widget is rendered in.</td>
	<td><tt>en</tt></td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td><tt>TabIndex</tt></td>
	<td>The tab position of the widget and its challenge. Set it on a page whose other fields use tab positions.</td>
	<td>unset</td>
</tr>
</table>

<p class="block-content">
The control passes these to the widget as written rather than checking them against a
list, so a value the service does not know is ignored by the service.
</p>

<com:TTextHighlighter Language="prado" CssClass="source block-content">
&lt;com:TReCaptcha2 ID="Captcha"
   SiteKey="..."
   SecretKey="..."
   Theme="dark"
   Size="compact"
   Language="fr" /&gt;
</com:TTextHighlighter>

<h2>Responding to the widget</h2>

<p class="block-content">
The widget reports back over a callback, so the page reacts without a full postback.
</p>

<table class="parameters">
<tr><th>Member</th><th>Purpose</th></tr>
<tr>
	<td><tt>OnCallback</tt></td>
	<td>Raised when the user solves the challenge. <tt>Response</tt> then holds the token the service issued.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td><tt>OnCallbackExpired</tt></td>
	<td>Raised when the token expires. The control clears <tt>Response</tt> and resets the widget before the handler runs.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td><tt>reset()</tt></td>
	<td>Clears the widget on the client, so the user is asked again.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td><tt>ResponseFieldName</tt></td>
	<td>The name of the posted field. A second control on the same page gets a suffixed name.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td><tt>WidgetId</tt></td>
	<td>The identifier the client library assigned to this widget.</td>
</tr>
</table>

